# Apex app paths — owner decision 2026-08-08: nikatru.com/<app> is the permanent
# public address for an app's commerce/product surface, so Paddle domain approval
# is a one-time act on the apex, never a per-app submission. The path serves the
# hub's per-app page today; the checkout increment upgrades it. That increment is
# gated by paywall.enabled=false (services/platform/src/app-config-data.json) plus
# OWNER_QUEUE A-1 -- NOT by OD-6.1, which CLOSED AS SUPERSEDED on 2026-08-08 (see
# knowledge/decisions/036-subly-freeze-dissolved-by-owner-order.md). This line named
# OD-6.1 as an open gate until 2026-08-11. sites/ sat outside that ADR's own declared
# citation sweep (company/pipeline/* and knowledge/), which is exactly why it survived.
# Precision, because a records file that overstates is the defect it is fixing: this
# file is public in the REPO but is never SERVED -- Cloudflare consumes _redirects and
# assert-web-cache-policy.mjs:168 lists it NOT_SERVED. pricing.html, repaired the same
# day, is the one whose comment shipped the stale gate id to every browser.
# The target is the EXTENSIONLESS form deliberately. Measured 2026-08-20:
# `/subscriptiontracker` -> 301 `/apps/subscriptiontracker.html` -> 308 `/apps/subscriptiontracker`, a double hop on the
# permanent commerce address named above. The second hop is documented Cloudflare
# behaviour, not a bug -- Pages "automatically redirects HTML files to
# extension-less versions"
# (developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/serving-pages/, 2026-08-20), so
# naming `.html` here BUYS a redirect. Pointing at `/apps/subscriptiontracker` serves 200 on the
# first hop. The file itself stays at apps/subscriptiontracker.html because the generator owns
# that path (tooling/sites/generate-discovery.mjs) and three limbs of
# assert-discovery-surface.mjs model `apps/<slug>.html`; relocating it to
# subscriptiontracker/index.html is the Phase 2 generator rework, not a redirect edit.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 🔴 RETIRED 2026-09-09 — THE TWO `/subscriptiontracker …` LINES ARE GONE, AND THIS FILE IS
# NOW EMPTY OF RULES. The whole record above is kept rather than deleted,
# because it is the record of a decision this one COMPLETES.
#
# The comment above is right about the destination and was right about the
# mechanism for the surface that existed when it was written: `nikatru.com/<app>`
# is the permanent public address, and Paddle approval is a one-time act on the
# apex. What it could not know is which surface would end up AT that path. It
# routed `/subscriptiontracker` to the MARKETING page because in August the app lived at
# `subly.nikatru.com` and the path had nothing else to serve.
#
# [ADR 075] moves the APP there. So these two lines stopped being a convenience
# and became a COLLISION: a 301 sitting on the exact prefix the apex router
# (`functions/_middleware.js`) now claims, on a project where `_redirects` is
# evaluated BEFORE Functions. Left in place they would have 301-ed every request
# for the running application to a marketing page — and the failure would have
# looked like "the app does not load", not like "a redirect rule won".
#
# `/apps/<id>` is UNCHANGED and stays the product page: it is where
# `tooling/sites/generate-discovery.mjs` writes it, where the sitemap, the hub,
# `llms.txt` and every store-facing link already point, and where
# `assert-discovery-surface.mjs` models it. Nothing that pointed at `/apps/subscriptiontracker`
# moves. What moves is `/subscriptiontracker`, from a redirect to the application itself.
#
# ⚠️ NOTHING REPLACES THESE LINES, and nothing should. `_redirects` cannot proxy
# — Cloudflare Pages supports 3xx and SAME-PROJECT 200 rewrites only, with no
# proxy-to-another-origin form — which is exactly why the router is a Pages
# Function and not two more lines here. Adding a per-app line to this file for a
# new app would put a redirect in front of the router again.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 🔴 TRANSITIONAL, AND IT IS DELETED BY THE VERY NEXT CHANGE. Added 2026-09-09
# to unblock the `subly` → `subscriptiontracker` slug rename, which cannot
# otherwise be landed at all. It is NOT a per-app line of the kind the paragraph
# above forbids: `/subscriptiontracker` is a path the apex router does not claim
# on this branch, and by the time it does, these two lines are gone.
#
# THE DEADLOCK THIS BREAKS, stated so the next reader does not re-derive it:
#   · `assert-catalog-reachable.mjs` refuses a catalogue that advertises a `live`
#     row whose URL does not answer. After the rename that URL is
#     `https://nikatru.com/subscriptiontracker`.
#   · That address can only answer once the APEX ROUTER learns the new path, and
#     the router is the `nikatru` Pages project, which is GIT-CONNECTED TO main.
#     So it cannot answer until the rename has already merged.
#   · CI on the rename branch therefore fails, `ci-gate` stays red, and
#     `deploy-web.yml`'s `assert-gate-passed.mjs` step refuses to run — which is
#     what would have created the new Cloudflare Pages project. Nothing moves.
#
# `preview` is what that guard suggests instead, and it was TRIED AND REJECTED on
# the same day: with zero live rows the discovery generator drops the app from
# the sitemap, llms.txt and the apps hub, and check-site-integrity.mjs then
# refuses the hand-maintained homepage array. `preview` means "never been live",
# not "moving house" — taking it would have pulled a working app off the public
# site for the length of a migration.
#
# 302, NOT 301: this must never be cached by a browser. The direction reverses
# within the hour — the rename commit deletes these two lines and adds the
# opposite pair (`/subly` → `/subscriptiontracker`, 308 permanent) in the SAME
# commit, so the two rules never coexist and no loop is possible.
#
# ⚠️ IF YOU ARE READING THIS AND `catalog/apps.json` ALREADY SAYS
# `subscriptiontracker`, THESE LINES ARE STALE AND ARE 302-ING THE LIVE APP TO A
# DEAD PATH. Delete them.
/subscriptiontracker     /subly          302
/subscriptiontracker/*   /subly/:splat   302
